 anyway getting  back to  the basics so i don't really and i've  said this before oh and another thing  too i'm trying to figure out the best  place to look  into my camera here and there's various  squares and circles  so i hope i'm doing it you know in a way  that is the least  distracting to people now getting back  to the subject  so when i did my learning indonesian  from scratch i didn't start from lesson  one  i started from lesson two and i said you  could just as easily start from lesson  five or less than 10  and go back to one or three or eight and  it really doesn't matter  and the reason is the following that  obviously some stories some content  is easier and some stories are easier  than others  and they are easier because they have a  lot of high  frequency vocabulary or vocabulary that  you  know that's familiar to you that's how  we design the mini stories  vocabulary repeats five times in a story  uh and we've tried to design the many  stories  so that you know in these 60 minutes  stories we have a lot of high  frequency verbs useful verbs give  go come take need want etc  so if you get a lot of repetition that's  going to make them easier  however the trouble with frequency as  i've said before is that it declines  very very quickly  so the high frequency vocabulary is  going to show up you needn't worry about  learning it  it will show up if you expose yourself  to enough  content that's not a concern the most  common structures in the language  will show up patterns like subject verb  object all of these things will appear  fairly soon  even if they are explained to you it  doesn't mean that you will  get it so to me  you know trying to nail down the basics  is somewhat  futile and probably unnecessary  certain things are basic for example so  and the other thing too is that in a  language  you know it's not like in math if you  don't have  sort of basic skills basic mathematical  skills  you can't do the next level of skill  i talked about swimming if you don't  have a basic swimming stroke  you know the ability to float then you  can't build on that to develop  more advanced swimming strokes or  swimming skills  but language is not like that language  is just words  it's not obvious that the present tense  is easier than the past tense it depends  on the language it depends how complex  these conjugations are  it's just words and the more you hear  the words the more the brain gets used  to the words the more  the brain gets used to different  structures the sooner it's going to  be able to operate in the language and  all of this is achieved as you know i  repeat this all the time  through massive input listening and  reading  and i tried to show you in the last  video how  even in the language that i don't know  just by exposing myself to enough of the  content  the language start the language will  start to seep into me and of course  the things that repeat the most often  which are going to be the high frequency  of verbs  or high frequency words they will be the  things that i start  to learn someone said what about  languages that use a completely  different script like indonesian uses  the latin alphabet  it makes it more difficult and you still  have to work on learning that script  but even there if you learn the script  so theoretically  you have been exposed to what this  symbol represents  if it's a an alphabet like the persian  or the arabic alphabet  that doesn't mean that you're  comfortable reading in that alphabet  or with the you know cyrillic alphabet  or the greek alphabet  or hangul in korean it doesn't mean  you're comfortable you still have to put  so much effort into reading so much  that the brain starts to get used to  something that  in a way is basic like the script if you  can't read the script you're not gonna  be able to read  so gaining some familiarity with the  script  if it's different from say the latin  alphabet in a way is basic  there's a situation where you do have  something that's basic where you have a  skill that's required in order to go  forward  whereas vocabulary it's more a matter of  of what you you know come across  in your listening and reading and  inevitably the  most frequent 70 of words are going to  show up and you're going to get to know  them fairly  soon so i think this this uh  this obsession with the basics and  building on the basics  basics in language learning is misplaced  it's not like  you have to learn the basics of math  before you can get into engineering  before you can build a bridge  and there's a lot of sort of knowledge  that builds on prior  knowledge in language if you were  totally dedicated to say  learning the terms for cooking in  persian because you were  only interested in persian food then you  would learn  all of the terms  that are connected to the cuisine and  those would be vocabulary items that  maybe for someone else would be  very difficult because they wouldn't see  them that often but if you were  constantly dealing with  recipes and how to make different dishes  in persian food or french food or  chinese food then those would be your  high frequency  words that you would learn so that there  are really no  basics you gradually become familiar now  at some point  you develop a curiosity why does the  language work this way versus  that way and at that point you can look  up explanations  but if you try to master the  explanations up front because  these are the basic structures of the  language or this is the basic  vocabulary and so i'm going to you know  get the most  you know the highest frequency 500 words  and learn them  in fact your brain won't get used to  them just by the fact that you  study these lists you still have to go  through the process  of listening and reading and allowing  your brain to get used to the language  so i i think i've said this before but  it's a theme that  that has kind of come back to me now  because of the fact that i did that  video about learning from scratch and i  reminded myself  of the fact that in fact i was having a  conversation with someone i think in  turkish or in persian  and my tutor said you know you only ever  talk about  you know economics and social problems  and politics uh what about  things from daily life in fact my  vocabulary  uh surrounding you know things in daily  life and food and clothing and stuff  was nowhere near as well developed as  this other  vocabulary because that's what i was  interested in that's what i was learning  that's what i was coming across so our  political terms  basic and carrot banana  strawberry less basic i don't know  it all depends on what you're interested  in you just have to expose yourself to  the language  and gradually allow the brain to get  used to it and don't try to nail down  the basics  i think it's a bit of a futile exercise  thank you for listening bye for now
 

